A||||2||WisconsinGT||Coolant pipe I think||||1
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||11-16-1999||05:20 PM||||Bare with me on this one. I will do my best to describe where the part is. I have a coolant leak on my 88gt. I thought it was an oil leak, but that is not the case. If you put yourself at the rear of the car looking at the engine, I need to know what the black pipe is near the bottom of the engine directly under the air filter. On my car it looks like it is cracked and peeling. I think the pipe connects the coolant pipe that runs under the car on the passengers side and the engine. Would anyone know how I could get a new one? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||mutfish||11-16-1999||07:48 PM||||That is a coolant pipe,and it's made of steel.Mine rusted through on my 88 gt this summer. I had to order one from my local Pontiac dealer.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||11-17-1999||02:07 PM||||I just replaced mine. The coolant pipe runs from the drivers side coolant hose, bolts to the transmission, extends along the front side of the block, just under the manifold. The other end turns straight up, just between the compressor and the corner of the valve cover. There is a hose coming from the thermostat housing, that attaches to this pipe. As I understand it, the 88s rusted much worse than the earlier ones. Per Pontiac... the 88 pipe was discontinued in February of this year. A dealer-initiated search turned up *nothing* anywhere in the US. I ended up buying a used 86 pipe, and modifying it to work. You have to saw off the last 4-5 inches. The angle is wrong. When you compare the pipes, you'll see what I mean. <BR>BTW... this is a real pain-in-the-ass job.<BR>Good luck.||||reg||1
